<article class="post">
  <div class="meta">Last updated: 28 October 2026</div>
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p>ColoringPageMaker needs no account and uses no cookies, analytics or advertising. This page lists the data the site handles, where it goes and why.</p>

  <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
  <p>ColoringPageMaker (www.coloringpagemaker.app) is run by Peak Apps Studio. For any question about your data, email <a href="mailto:info@peakappsstudio.com">info@peakappsstudio.com</a>.</p>

  <h2>When you make a page</h2>
  <p>When you press generate, your browser sends your description and the detail level you chose to our server. The server adds its own drawing instructions and sends the text to an image model, which draws the page. The image comes back to your browser. We do not save your descriptions or your images. Which service draws the page depends on the page:</p>
  <ul class="post-list-items">
    <li>Free pages, and Pro pages in standard quality, are drawn by Workers AI, a service of Cloudflare, with the FLUX.1 [schnell] model. Cloudflare's Workers AI data usage terms say that it does not retain the text or the picture after the request, with limited exceptions listed there, and that it does not use them to train models. Cloudflare Images then turns the picture gray.</li>
    <li>Pro pages in high quality (HD) are drawn by OpenAI's image service while the site's HD budget lasts and, for purchases from 21 October 2026, while your monthly HD allowance lasts. When either is used up, the page is drawn in standard quality by Workers AI. OpenAI receives the request from our server, not your IP address or browser details.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Please do not include a child's name or other personal details in your descriptions. Under OpenAI's API data policy, data sent through the API is kept for up to 30 days to monitor abuse and, by default, is not used to train OpenAI's models.</p>

  <h2>Daily limits and your IP address</h2>
  <p>Free pages are limited to two a day, and the limit resets at midnight UTC. To count them, our server turns your IP address into a one-way hash, mixed with a secret value that only we hold, and stores a counter under that hash. The address itself is not stored with the counter. The counters live in the site's own database on Cloudflare (a Durable Object with SQLite storage) and are deleted automatically about two days after they were last changed. For Pro, the daily counter is stored under your Gumroad sale ID instead, and a second counter under the same ID counts your HD pages for the month and is deleted about 40 days after it was last changed.</p>
  <p>The same database keeps a few totals that belong to no one: the free pages of the day for all visitors together, the images drawn that day, and the OpenAI spend of the day and the month.</p>

  <h2>PDF downloads</h2>
  <p>On the home page, the PDF is built in your browser, and the PDF library loads from cdnjs, a public code library run by Cloudflare. On the theme pages and the printable pages, your browser sends the images for the PDF to our server, which builds the PDF in memory, sends it back and does not keep a copy.</p>

  <h2>What is stored in your browser</h2>
  <ul class="post-list-items">
    <li>The pages you made in this visit are kept in your browser's session storage so they survive a page reload. They are deleted when you close the tab.</li>
    <li>When you open the site, it notes in session storage what kind of site sent you, as one word such as "google", "pinterest", "social" or "direct" when no site did. It reads this from the page you came from, or from a tag in the link you followed, such as utm_source=chatgpt.com. If neither says, the kind of app you opened the page in, for example Pinterest or Instagram, counts the same way. It keeps only that word, never an address, and the word is deleted when you close the tab.</li>
    <li>The first time another site sends you here, that word is also saved in your browser's local storage, so that a later visit can still be credited to the site that first sent you. It is saved once and never replaced, it stays until you clear this site's data, and it is never an address. Neither word goes anywhere unless you click a Pro button, as explained below.</li>
    <li>If you activate Pro, a Pro token is saved in your browser's local storage so that Pro stays on. The token is a random code made by our server. It does not contain your email address or your license key. To remove it, press the "Pro active" button on the home page and confirm "Sign out of Pro on this device", which also deletes the token from our database, or clear this site's data.</li>
    <li>Earlier versions of the site saved your Pro email address in your browser instead. The site now deletes it the next time you open one of its pages, and keeps only a note to ask you for your license key.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pro, your license key and your email address</h2>
  <p>Pro is sold through Gumroad. The buy buttons open Gumroad's checkout, where you give your payment details to Gumroad, not to us.</p>
  <p>When you click a Pro button, it goes to Gumroad through a tracking link that tells Gumroad what kind of site first sent you to us, for example a search engine, an AI assistant, Pinterest, a social network, another website or none. Gumroad learns only the kind of site, never its address. We use it to see which kinds of sites bring buyers. Until you click, these words stay in your browser, and this site still uses no cookies and no analytics.</p>
  <p>Pro turns on only with the license key from your Gumroad receipt. When you enter it, our server checks it with Gumroad's license service (api.gumroad.com). If Gumroad confirms a Pro purchase that has not been refunded, the server makes a random Pro token and sends it to your browser. In our database it stores the token with your purchase email address, the Gumroad sale ID and the date, and deletes this record automatically about 400 days later, or when you sign out on that device. After that you can turn Pro on again with the same key. We do not store your license key. An email address on its own never turns Pro on, and the site never tells anyone whether an email address bought Pro.</p>
  <p>Your browser sends the Pro token with each page you generate or download, so the server can apply the Pro limits. When Gumroad notifies our server of a sale, the server checks that sale's license key in the same way and keeps a purchase record: your email address, the Gumroad sale ID and the date. If Gumroad reports a refund or chargeback to our server, the server deletes that record and stops the Pro tokens made from that sale. To do this it keeps the sale ID, marked as refunded, for about 400 days. As the seller, we also see your email address and order details in Gumroad, and we use them to answer questions and issue refunds.</p>

  <h2>Hosting and fonts</h2>
  <p>The site runs on Cloudflare. Cloudflare's network serves the pages, images and fonts, and a Cloudflare Worker (our server code) handles drawing, PDFs, Pro and the other requests that need code. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes each request, including your IP address, the page you asked for and your browser type, and keeps network logs under its own policy. For the requests our code handles, Cloudflare's Workers Logs also records the request (its address, method and result, plus request details from Cloudflare such as headers and country, which can contain your IP address) and the technical error messages our code writes. These logs are kept for three days. Our code does not write your descriptions or your images to them. The fonts are served from this site, so no font service sees your visit.</p>

  <h2>Pro tokens from before the move to Cloudflare</h2>
  <p>Pro tokens made before the site moved to Cloudflare are not in the new database. When our server meets a token it does not know, it asks the site's previous server, which still runs on Vercel, whether the token is valid. The request carries only the token. It does not carry your IP address, your description or your browser details. The previous server looks the token up in its database at Upstash. If the answer is yes, the token is copied into the Cloudflare database without an email address and is not asked about again. If the answer is no, our server remembers a one-way hash of the token for one day so that it does not ask again. The previous server and its database still hold what they held before the move: Pro tokens with the purchase email address, Gumroad sale ID and date, and purchase records, for the periods listed under How long data is kept.</p>

  <h2>Pinterest</h2>
  <p>A scheduled job on our server draws printable coloring pages from topics we choose, not from anything visitors type, and publishes them in a feed that Pinterest reads. No visitor data goes into the feed. Some pages carry a Save to Pinterest link. If you press it, your browser opens Pinterest with the address of that page, and Pinterest handles the rest under its own policy. We send Pinterest nothing else. When the feed is requested, our server keeps a short log of the latest 60 requests: the time, which feed, the number of pins returned and the first 120 characters of the requester's user agent. It keeps no IP address.</p>

  <h2>Emails</h2>
  <p>If you email us, we receive your email address and your message. Mail sent to peakappsstudio.com addresses is forwarded by Forward Email to a Gmail inbox. We use your email only to reply to you.</p>

  <h2>Who else receives data</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Company</th><th scope="col">What for</th><th scope="col">Privacy policy</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
